00:00:02.470 --> 00:00:04.309 align:start position:0%
the government promised that businesses  closed under local lockdowns would get  financial support  and after weeks of wrangling they have  finalized deals with  four regions placed into tier three  restrictions now these  are the deals around a third of the  total you see here is for support with  test and trace the rest  is for businesses south yorkshire will  get 41 million pounds that's the  equivalent of 29 pounds  per person lancashire gets 42 million  pounds 28 pounds  for every citizen liverpool 44 million  pounds around 27 pounds 50  and greater manchester will now receive  82 million pounds  in total that's 29 pounds per head now  we know mayor andy burnham was pushing  for a better deal and that talks  founded on a gap of just 5 million  pounds  so why in the middle of a health  emergency is the government quibbling  over such a relatively tiny figure  the public finances might help explain  coronavirus has sent public borrowing  soaring  here in light blue is last year's  monthly borrowing figures  the dark blue is this year's you can see  how it actually fell  back in the summer as the virus abated  but in september it rose the government  borrowing was 36.1  billion pounds that's 28 billion pounds  more  than last year now 5.9 billion of that  was spent on the furlough  and self-employment support schemes  remember furlough  ends in 10 days and will be replaced  with the cheaper  job support scheme now that spending  helped take public sector net  borrowing to 208 billion pounds  for the past six months alone and it  pushed the national debt  even higher well above 2 trillion pounds  to 103.5  of gdp that's the highest debt ratio  since the 60s but you can see it's  nothing  like as high as it was back at the end  of world war  ii now the chancellor's problem is just  as  he wants to cut the spending the virus  has come  roaring back and calls for more support  not less  will only grow as tier three  restrictions move  south the picture is so uncertain that  he's cancelled  the planned three-year spending review  instead it will cover just one year  and some of those spending commitments  may not last that long  what is certain is that pressure on the  chancellor to spend more  not less on supporting jobs and  businesses will grow  as long as lockdowns are central to  government strategy
 

